Lab: Write Loops and Conditional Tasks in Ansible
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Implement loop constructs in Ansible playbooks to manage repetitive tasks
Apply when conditionals to control task execution based on system facts and variables
Combine loops and conditionals to build efficient, real-world automation
Use register and failed_when/failed_when: false to handle errors explicitly instead of masking them
Debug loop and conditional logic using the debug module
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Ansible fundamentals (inventory, playbooks, modules)
Knowledge of YAML syntax
Familiarity with Linux command line operations
Access to a control node and at least one managed node with SSH connectivity
Install Ansible on the control node if it is not already present:

sudo apt update && sudo apt install -y ansible
# or on RHEL/CentOS:
# sudo yum install -y ansible
ansible --version
This lab assumes the managed node runs Ubuntu (Debian family). All package tasks target apt.

Lab Environment
Ready-to-Use Cloud Machines: This lab is intended for a pre-configured, isolated practice environment (for example, an Al Nafi cloud machine or your own disposable VM pair). Do not run these playbooks against production infrastructure.

Your lab environment includes:

Control Node: A Linux system with Ansible installed
Managed Node: An Ubuntu 20.04+ system reachable over SSH
SSH key-based access configured between the two nodes
Task 1: Set Up the Lab and Implement Basic Loops
Subtask 1.1: Set Up Lab Directory Structure
Connect to your control node and create the lab directory:
mkdir -p ~/ansible-loops-conditionals
cd ~/ansible-loops-conditionals
Create the inventory file (replace <MANAGED_NODE_IP> with your managed node's address):
cat > inventory << 'EOF'
[webservers]
managed-node ansible_host=<MANAGED_NODE_IP> ansible_user=ubuntu

[all:vars]
ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/.ssh/id_rsa
ansible_ssh_common_args='-o StrictHostKeyChecking=no'
EOF
Test connectivity:
ansible all -i inventory -m ping
Deliverable: The ping command returns "ping": "pong" with a SUCCESS status for managed-node. If this fails, resolve SSH connectivity before continuing.

Subtask 1.2: Basic Loop Implementation
Create a basic loop playbook:
cat > basic-loops.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Basic Loops Demonstration
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  tasks:

    - name: Create multiple directories using loop
      file:
        path: "/tmp/{{ item }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop:
        - app1
        - app2
        - app3
        - logs
        - config

    - name: Create multiple files with different content and modes
      copy:
        content: "This is the {{ item.name }} file.\n"
        dest: "/tmp/{{ item.name }}.txt"
        mode: "{{ item.mode }}"
      loop:
        - { name: "readme", mode: "0644" }
        - { name: "script", mode: "0755" }
        - { name: "config", mode: "0600" }

    - name: Display loop results
      debug:
        msg: "Created file: {{ item.name }} with mode {{ item.mode }}"
      loop:
        - { name: "readme", mode: "0644" }
        - { name: "script", mode: "0755" }
        - { name: "config", mode: "0600" }
EOF
Execute the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory basic-loops.yml
Verify the results on the managed node:
ansible webservers -i inventory -m shell -a "ls -la /tmp/*.txt"
ansible webservers -i inventory -m shell -a "ls -ld /tmp/app1 /tmp/app2 /tmp/app3"
Deliverable: The playbook run shows changed for the directory and file creation tasks, and the debug task prints three messages, one per file, showing the correct mode for each. The ls verification commands confirm the files and directories exist with the expected permissions (-rw-r--r-- for readme, -rwxr-xr-x for script, -rw------- for config).

Subtask 1.3: Installing Multiple Packages with Loops
Create a package installation playbook:
cat > package-loops.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Install Multiple Packages Using Loops
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    web_packages:
      - curl
      - wget
      - unzip
    dev_packages:
      - git
      - vim
      - htop
      - tree

  tasks:

    - name: Update package cache
      apt:
        update_cache: yes
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"

    - name: Install web utility packages
      apt:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ web_packages }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"

    - name: Install development packages
      apt:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ dev_packages }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"

    - name: Verify installed packages using dpkg-query
      command: "dpkg-query -W -f='${Status}' {{ item }}"
      loop: "{{ web_packages + dev_packages }}"
      register: package_status
      changed_when: false
      failed_when: false

    - name: Display package installation status
      debug:
        msg: "Package {{ item.item }} status: {{ 'INSTALLED' if 'install ok installed' in item.stdout else 'NOT FOUND' }}"
      loop: "{{ package_status.results }}"
EOF
Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory package-loops.yml
Deliverable: Every package in web_packages and dev_packages reports INSTALLED in the final debug output. Because failed_when: false is used explicitly on the verification task (rather than a blanket ignore_errors), a package that fails to query will show as NOT FOUND without hiding real installation failures earlier in the play.

Task 2: Implement Conditional Tasks
Subtask 2.1: Basic Conditionals
Create a basic conditionals playbook:
cat > basic-conditionals.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Basic Conditionals Demonstration
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    app_environment: "production"
    enable_monitoring: true
    server_role: "webserver"

  tasks:

    - name: Gather system facts
      setup:

    - name: Install nginx only on Ubuntu systems
      apt:
        name: nginx
        state: present
      when: ansible_distribution == "Ubuntu"

    - name: Install httpd on RedHat family systems
      yum:
        name: httpd
        state: present
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"

    - name: Write production configuration
      copy:
        content: |
          # Production Configuration
          server_name = {{ ansible_hostname }}
          environment = {{ app_environment }}
          debug = false
        dest: /tmp/app.conf
      when: app_environment == "production"

    - name: Write development configuration
      copy:
        content: |
          # Development Configuration
          server_name = {{ ansible_hostname }}
          environment = {{ app_environment }}
          debug = true
        dest: /tmp/app.conf
      when: app_environment == "development"

    - name: Install monitoring tools when enabled
      apt:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop:
        - htop
        - iotop
        - nethogs
      when:
        - enable_monitoring | bool
        - ansible_os_family == "Debian"

    - name: Create web directory for web servers
      file:
        path: /var/www/html/app
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      when: server_role == "webserver"

    - name: Display conditional results
      debug:
        msg: |
          System: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}
          Environment: {{ app_environment }}
          Monitoring: {{ enable_monitoring }}
          Role: {{ server_role }}
EOF
Execute the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory basic-conditionals.yml
Deliverable: On an Ubuntu managed node, the nginx task shows changed while the httpd task shows skipping. /tmp/app.conf is created with debug = false since app_environment is production. The final debug message prints the correct OS, environment, monitoring flag, and role.

Subtask 2.2: Advanced Conditionals with Multiple Criteria
Create an advanced conditionals playbook:
cat > advanced-conditionals.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Advanced Conditionals with Multiple Criteria
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    min_memory_gb: 1
    required_disk_space_gb: 5
    supported_os_versions:
      - "18.04"
      - "20.04"
      - "22.04"

  tasks:

    - name: Gather system facts
      setup:

    - name: Report memory size
      debug:
        msg: "System has {{ (ansible_memtotal_mb / 1024) | round(1) }}GB RAM"

    - name: Install memory-aware packages when requirements are met
      apt:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop:
        - nodejs
        - npm
      when:
        - (ansible_memtotal_mb / 1024) >= min_memory_gb
        - ansible_distribution == "Ubuntu"
        - ansible_distribution_version in supported_os_versions

    - name: Check available disk space on root filesystem
      shell: "set -o pipefail && df -BG / | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}' | tr -d 'G'"
      args:
        executable: /bin/bash
      register: available_space
      changed_when: false

    - name: Create backup directory only if enough disk space is available
      file:
        path: /tmp/backup
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      when: available_space.stdout | int >= required_disk_space_gb

    - name: Display system compatibility report
      debug:
        msg: |
          === System Compatibility Report ===
          OS: {{ ansible_distribution }} {{ ansible_distribution_version }}
          Memory: {{ (ansible_memtotal_mb / 1024) | round(1) }}GB (Required: {{ min_memory_gb }}GB)
          Available Disk: {{ available_space.stdout }}GB (Required: {{ required_disk_space_gb }}GB)
          OS Supported: {{ ansible_distribution_version in supported_os_versions }}
          Memory OK: {{ (ansible_memtotal_mb / 1024) >= min_memory_gb }}
          Disk OK: {{ available_space.stdout | int >= required_disk_space_gb }}
EOF
Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory advanced-conditionals.yml
Deliverable: The compatibility report in the final debug task shows True for OS Supported, Memory OK, and Disk OK on a standard Ubuntu 20.04 VM with at least 1GB RAM and 5GB free disk space. The /tmp/backup directory is created only when the disk check passes.

Task 3: Combine Loops and Conditionals with Explicit Error Handling
Subtask 3.1: Package Installation with Explicit Prerequisite Checks
Rather than using a blanket ignore_errors: yes (which hides real failures), this task checks prerequisites explicitly and uses failed_when: false only where a non-zero result is an expected, valid outcome.

Create the combined playbook:
cat > loops-conditionals-combined.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Combined Loops and Conditionals with Explicit Error Handling
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    packages_to_check:
      - name: "curl"
        critical: true
      - name: "wget"
        critical: true
      - name: "nonexistent-demo-package"
        critical: false
      - name: "vim"
        critical: true

  tasks:

    - name: Gather system facts
      setup:

    - name: Check package availability in apt cache before installing
      command: "apt-cache show {{ item.name }}"
      loop: "{{ packages_to_check }}"
      register: cache_check
      changed_when: false
      # A non-zero return code here means the package does not exist in the
      # cache. That is an expected, valid outcome for the deliberately fake
      # package, so we record it explicitly instead of masking it.
      failed_when: false

    - name: Install packages that exist in the apt cache
      apt:
        name: "{{ item.item.name }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ cache_check.results }}"
      when: item.rc == 0

    - name: Report packages that could not be found
      debug:
        msg: "SKIPPED: {{ item.item.name }} was not found in the apt cache (critical={{ item.item.critical }})"
      loop: "{{ cache_check.results }}"
      when: item.rc != 0

    - name: Fail the play if a critical package was unavailable
      fail:
        msg: "Critical package {{ item.item.name }} is missing and cannot be installed."
      loop: "{{ cache_check.results }}"
      when:
        - item.rc != 0
        - item.item.critical | bool

    - name: Display final package status summary
      debug:
        msg: |
          Package: {{ item.item.name }}
          Critical: {{ item.item.critical }}
          Found in cache: {{ item.rc == 0 }}
      loop: "{{ cache_check.results }}"
EOF
Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory loops-conditionals-combined.yml
Deliverable: The play completes successfully. curl, wget, and vim install normally, nonexistent-demo-package is reported as SKIPPED with a clear reason, and because it is marked critical: false, the play does not fail. Every use of failed_when: false is paired with a comment explaining exactly which non-zero result is expected and why, rather than a blanket ignore_errors: yes.

Subtask 3.2: Deployment Summary with Loops Over Multiple Facts
Create a deployment summary playbook that loops over application definitions and reports readiness using conditionals:
cat > deployment-summary.yml << 'EOF'
---
- name: Deployment Readiness Summary
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    web_apps:
      - name: "frontend"
        port: 3000
        min_memory_mb: 256
      - name: "api"
        port: 8080
        min_memory_mb: 256
      - name: "admin"
        port: 9000
        min_memory_mb: 128

    monitoring_enabled: true
    ssl_enabled: false
    backup_enabled: true
    backup_paths:
      - "/var/www"
      - "/etc/nginx"

  tasks:

    - name: Gather system facts
      setup:

    - name: Create application placeholder directories
      file:
        path: "/tmp/apps/{{ item.name }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'
      loop: "{{ web_apps }}"
      when: ansible_memtotal_mb >= item.min_memory_mb

    - name: Report applications that do not meet memory requirements
      debug:
        msg: "WARNING: {{ item.name }} requires {{ item.min_memory_mb }}MB but system only has {{ ansible_memtotal_mb }}MB"
      loop: "{{ web_apps }}"
      when: ansible_memtotal_mb < item.min_memory_mb

    - name: Create backup directories when backups are enabled
      file:
        path: "/tmp/backup/{{ item | basename }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0700'
      loop: "{{ backup_paths }}"
      when: backup_enabled | bool

    - name: Display deployment summary
      debug:
        msg: |
          === Deployment Summary ===
          Applications evaluated: {{ web_apps | length }}
          Applications ready: {{ web_apps | selectattr('min_memory_mb', 'le', ansible_memtotal_mb) | list | length }}
          Backup paths configured: {{ backup_paths | length if backup_enabled else 0 }}
          SSL: {{ "Enabled" if ssl_enabled else "Disabled" }}
          Monitoring: {{ "Enabled" if monitoring_enabled else "Disabled" }}
EOF
Run the playbook:
ansible-playbook -i inventory deployment-summary.yml
Deliverable: The final debug task prints a summary block showing the count of applications evaluated, how many meet the memory requirement (all three, on a typical lab VM with at least 512MB RAM), the number of backup paths configured, and the literal strings SSL: Disabled and Monitoring: Enabled reflecting the variable values. Directories for each app appear under /tmp/apps/ and backup directories appear under /tmp/backup/.

Expected Outcomes
You can write and execute Ansible playbooks that use loop to manage lists and dictionaries of items
You can control task execution with when conditionals based on facts, registered variables, and custom variables
You can replace blanket ignore_errors: yes with explicit prerequisite checks and documented failed_when: false conditions, producing clearer and more trustworthy automation
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Loop variable shows as undefined or renders literally as {{ item }} Symptom: The debug output prints {{ item }} instead of a value, or Ansible raises an 'item' is undefined error. Diagnostic hint: Check that the task actually has a loop: key at the same indentation level as the module, and confirm you are not using loop_control.loop_var with a different variable name while still referencing item in the task.

Issue 2: A when condition does not behave as expected Symptom: A task runs when you expected it to skip, or vice versa. Diagnostic hint: Run the playbook with -vv to see the evaluated condition, and confirm the data types being compared (for example, a fact might be a string like "20.04" while your variable is a list of strings, or a boolean variable might actually be a string like "true" requiring the | bool filter).

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a series of Ansible playbooks that progressively introduced loop constructs, conditional logic, and the combination of both to automate realistic system administration tasks such as package installation, configuration management, and deployment readiness checks. You practiced looping over simple lists, lists of dictionaries, and registered results, and you used conditionals driven by Ansible facts, custom variables, and command output to control exactly when tasks execute. You also replaced blanket ignore_errors: yes usage with explicit prerequisite checks and documented failed_when: false conditions, which is a more maintainable and honest way to handle expected failure cases in production-quality playbooks. To continue building on these skills, practice writing custom Jinja2 templates driven by loop variables, explore loop_control options such as label for cleaner output, and study Ansible's block/rescue/always error-handling constructs as a complement to conditional logic.Xy

